---
import Lesson from '../../layouts/LessonLayout.astro';

const body = `<h1>4.4 — Nested Loops: The GridArchitect &amp; Fog-of-War Build</h1>

<p>
    <strong>CED Topic 2.11 · 90-Minute Unified Build.</strong> When one loop sits inside another,
    the inner loop runs to completion for <em>every single step</em> of the outer loop. Today you
    will build <code>GridArchitect.java</code> — generating bordered ASCII dungeon rooms, Manhattan-distance
    fog-of-war maps, dependent staircase patterns, and unique item-pair combinations.
</p>

<div class="callout">
    <strong>Starting Files (Download Both):</strong>
    <ul>
        <li>
            <a href="../data/GridArchitect.java" download>GridArchitect.java</a> — bare-minimum
            compiling stub.
        </li>
        <li>
            <a href="../data/GridArchitectCheck.java" download>GridArchitectCheck.java</a> —
            automated AP-style test suite (13 checks across 4 parts).
        </li>
    </ul>
    <pre><code>javac GridArchitect.java GridArchitectCheck.java
java GridArchitectCheck</code></pre>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity expand-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Parts 1 &amp; 2 · 2D Coordinate Grids</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~45 min</span>
    </div>

    <h3>Part 1: <code>buildBorderedRoom(int rows, int cols)</code></h3>
    <p>
        Return a multi-line <code>String</code> representing a <code>rows</code> by
        <code>cols</code> dungeon room (<code>0 .. rows - 1</code> and <code>0 .. cols - 1</code>):
    </p>
    <ul>
        <li>Every cell on the outer boundary (first row <code>r == 0</code>, last row
            <code>r == rows - 1</code>, first column <code>c == 0</code>, or last column
            <code>c == cols - 1</code>) is a wall (<code>"#"</code>).</li>
        <li>Every interior cell is floor (<code>"."</code>).</li>
        <li>Append a newline (<code>"\\n"</code>) at the end of each row (right after the inner
            column loop finishes).</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Part 2: <code>renderFogOfWar(int size, int heroRow, int heroCol, int visionRadius)</code></h3>
    <p>
        Render a <code>size</code> by <code>size</code> grid (rows <code>0 .. size - 1</code>,
        columns <code>0 .. size - 1</code>) around the hero at <code>(heroRow, heroCol)</code>:
    </p>
    <ul>
        <li>The hero's exact tile <code>(r == heroRow &amp;&amp; c == heroCol)</code> is
            <code>"@"</code>.</li>
        <li>Any other tile whose <strong>Manhattan distance</strong> from the hero
            (<code>Math.abs(r - heroRow) + Math.abs(c - heroCol)</code>) is
            <code>&lt;= visionRadius</code> is visible floor (<code>"."</code>).</li>
        <li>All tiles beyond <code>visionRadius</code> are shrouded in fog (<code>"?"</code>).</li>
        <li>Append <code>"\\n"</code> at the end of every row.</li>
    </ul>

    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Socratic Checkpoint — Row-Break Placement &amp; Manhattan Diamonds</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>What happens to the returned string if you accidentally place
                <code>result += "\\n";</code> <em>inside</em> the inner column loop instead of
                between the end of the inner loop and the end of the outer loop?</li>
            <li>Why does the Manhattan distance condition
                <code>Math.abs(r - heroRow) + Math.abs(c - heroCol) &lt;= 1</code> produce a
                5-tile diamond (up, down, left, right, center) rather than a 3×3 square? What
                is the Manhattan distance of a diagonal corner like
                <code>(heroRow - 1, heroCol - 1)</code>?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity expand-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Parts 3 &amp; 4 · Discovery &amp; Build</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~45 min</span>
    </div>

    <h3>Part 3: <code>buildRightStaircase(int height)</code></h3>
    <p>
        For each row <code>r</code> from <code>1</code> through <code>height</code> (return
        <code>""</code> if <code>height &lt;= 0</code>), append <code>height - r</code> dots
        (<code>"."</code>) followed by <code>r</code> hashes (<code>"#"</code>) and a newline
        (<code>"\\n"</code>). For <code>height = 4</code>:
    </p>
    <pre><code>...#
..##
.###
####</code></pre>

    <h3>Part 4: <code>countLuckyLootPairs(int maxTier)</code></h3>
    <p>
        Two treasure chests are forged with integer tiers <code>a</code> and <code>b</code> between
        <code>1</code> and <code>maxTier</code> inclusive. A pair of <strong>distinct</strong>
        tiers is a "Lucky Pair" if <code>(a + b) % 3 == 0</code>, where order does not matter
        (so <code>(1, 2)</code> and <code>(2, 1)</code> are the <em>same</em> pair and must only
        be counted once, and a tier cannot pair with itself like <code>(3, 3)</code>).
    </p>

    <div class="bug-hunt">
        <h3>Discovery Trap: The Self-Pair &amp; Double-Count Bug</h3>
        <p>
            Look at what happens when a student starts both loops at <code>1</code>:
        </p>
        <pre><code>// Naive nested loop — why does this return 5 instead of 2 for maxTier = 4?
int count = 0;
for (int a = 1; a &lt;= maxTier; a++) {
    for (int b = 1; b &lt;= maxTier; b++) {
        if ((a + b) % 3 == 0) {
            count++;
        }
    }
}</code></pre>
        <ol>
            <li>List all 5 pairs <code>(a, b)</code> that the naive loop above counts when
                <code>maxTier = 4</code>. Which ones are self-pairs (<code>a == b</code>), and
                which ones are reversed duplicates of an earlier pair?</li>
            <li>How does starting the inner loop at <code>int b = a + 1;</code> automatically
                prevent <em>both</em> self-pairs and reversed duplicates in a single stroke?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="checklist">
        <h3>Done When</h3>
        <ul>
            <li><code>java GridArchitectCheck</code> reports <strong><code>13 passed, 0 failed</code></strong></li>
            <li>You have completed the reflection boxes above and downloaded your <code>.json</code> for Canvas</li>
        </ul>
    </div>
</div>

<div class="resources">
    <h3>Reference Docs</h3>
    <ul>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-04/nested-loops.html">Nested Loops</a> — outer/inner mechanics, dependent bounds</li>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-02/math-class.html">Math Class</a> — Math.abs() for Manhattan distance</li>
    </ul>
</div>`;
